{
    "word": "taoism",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "a5e09749-2b37-3787-888e-ec06dc73a5e3",
                "word": "Taoism",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "67b9c1e1-3f6c-3e21-9115-8a9555ff86a9",
                        "entry_id": "a5e09749-2b37-3787-888e-ec06dc73a5e3",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A Chinese mystical philosophy traditionally founded by Lao-tzu in the 6th century B.C.E. that teaches conformity to the tao by unassertive action and simplicity."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "b26b3916-00b1-4644-9124-e865c7a852f2",
                                "sense_id": "67b9c1e1-3f6c-3e21-9115-8a9555ff86a9",
                                "text": "The philosophical Taoism of the “Tao Te Ching” seeks to remain connected to this “mother of the world,” the formless Tao (meaning “Way” or “Course”), that is seemingly the opposite of all we value, but is actually the source of all we value, as manure is to flowers, as the emptiness of a womb is to the fullness of life."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "759f9e3b-e7d7-49aa-9c2b-ce71f78c9313",
                                "sense_id": "67b9c1e1-3f6c-3e21-9115-8a9555ff86a9",
                                "text": "Taoism puts a significant emphasis on noninterference, imbuing the decision not to act with moral quality. As a result, Taoism seems like a virtue ethic, with wu-wei as its chief virtue."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b391a61e-60a3-3826-9d25-20f84baf2f0c",
                        "entry_id": "a5e09749-2b37-3787-888e-ec06dc73a5e3",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A religion developed from Taoist philosophy and folk and Buddhist religion and concerned with obtaining long life and good fortune often by magical means."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "258c8f4c-ba34-42fa-a6d0-f21d28146a09",
                                "sense_id": "b391a61e-60a3-3826-9d25-20f84baf2f0c",
                                "text": "The Chinese New Year holiday period, being disrupted this year by the outbreak of a viral illness, has evolved over more than 3,000 years to become the most important of China’s traditional festivals.\nAs celebrated in China and in many other places where its known as the Lunar New Year, it is largely a secular holiday, yet it includes rituals and traditions that derive from Confucianism, Buddhism and Taoism, as well as from ancient myths and folk religions."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ae4b3db6-df49-32a7-a582-ec2cd8a6129f",
                        "entry_id": "a5e09749-2b37-3787-888e-ec06dc73a5e3",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Traditional Chinese folk religion"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Singapore"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "844f9736-3822-4f62-bf17-b2cd5ba19a1c",
                                "sense_id": "ae4b3db6-df49-32a7-a582-ec2cd8a6129f",
                                "text": "Buddhism is the most followed religion in Singapore, with over 40 percent of the population declaring themselves believers, according to the latest census. Most of these practice a form of the religion that incorporates elements of Taoism and traditional Chinese faiths."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
